Let js2c name the built-in scripts the same way on Windows

node_javascript.cc holds every built-in JavaScript file as a C array, and the name of
each array is made from the file's path: "deps/acorn/acorn_walk/dist/walk.mjs" becomes
internal_deps_acorn_acorn_walk_dist_walk_raw. node.gyp lists those paths with forward
slashes, but on Windows ninja hands the action's arguments over in a response file and
gyp normalizes separators while writing it, so js2c is given

    deps\acorn\acorn_walk\dist\walk.mjs

the "deps/" prefix is no longer recognised, the backslashes are not turned into "_"
either, and the generated C++ reads

    static const uint8_t deps\acorn\acorn_walk\dist\walk_raw[] = { ... };

which clang-cl rejects ("default initialization of an object of const type", "expected
';' after top level declarator", "redefinition of 'deps' with a different type"). Node's
own Windows build does not hit this because it uses the MSBuild generator, whose action
command lines never go through that normalisation - the same reason three earlier
patches were needed for torque and for response-file arguments.

Separators are canonicalized once, where the command line is read, so the prefix
stripping and the identifier are computed from the spelling the build files use.
Nothing changes elsewhere: a path with no backslash is returned unchanged.

diff -ruN a/tools/js2c.cc b/tools/js2c.cc
--- a/tools/js2c.cc	2026-11-11 00:20:20.000000000 +0000
+++ b/tools/js2c.cc	2026-11-17 04:05:59.694750271 +0000
@@ -867,6 +867,23 @@
   return 1;
 }
 
+// Windows builds drive this tool from a ninja response file, and gyp normalizes
+// separators while writing one, so a file node.gyp listed as
+// "deps/acorn/acorn_walk/dist/walk.mjs" arrives as "deps\acorn\acorn_walk\dist\walk.mjs".
+// The name every file is registered under is made from that string - GetIdFromFilename
+// strips "deps/" and GetVariableName turns separators into "_" - so the backslash
+// spelling is not stripped, survives into the generated C++, and is not a valid
+// identifier. Forward slashes are the spelling the build files use, and Windows
+// accepts them for opening a file, so the tool sees one spelling everywhere.
+std::string UseForwardSlashes(std::string path) {
+  for (char& c : path) {
+    if (c == '\\') {
+      c = '/';
+    }
+  }
+  return path;
+}
+
 int Main(int argc, char* argv[]) {
   if (argc < 3) {
     return PrintUsage(argv[0]);
@@ -886,7 +903,7 @@
       }
       root_dir = argv[++i];
     } else {
-      args.emplace_back(argv[i]);
+      args.emplace_back(UseForwardSlashes(argv[i]));
     }
   }
 
